IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `cbca-core`

-- Canada Business Corporations Act (R.S.C. 1985, c. C-44), piece cb1 -- THE LOCAL NOUNS.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL. Encoded from a plain-text rendering of the Justice Canada consolidation current to
-- 2026-09-30, which is not an official version; nothing here is verified against the official text.
--
-- WHAT THIS MODULE IS. Only the nouns that two or more of the cb1 modules (s 3 to s 47) share, and no
-- rule. The vocabulary of the whole Act is in cbca-core.l4 and is not repeated here. Every name below
-- carries a marker of its own range ("Parts I to VI", "Part IV", "Part V") so that it cannot clash with a
-- name written by the piece that encodes Parts VII onward.

§ `cb1: the local nouns`

§§ `The most a court may impose for an offence in Parts I to VI`

-- The Act states a punishment as "a fine not exceeding $X", with or without "or to imprisonment for a term
-- not exceeding N", on summary conviction or on indictment. `maximum imprisonment in months` is NOTHING
-- where the provision names no imprisonment at all: that is the Act's answer for those offences, not a gap.
-- Years are written as months (five years = 60 months).
-- src:270 | (6) A corporation that, without reasonable cause, fails to comply with this section is guilty of an offence and liable on summary conviction to a fine not exceeding five thousand dollars.
-- src:417 | (b) on conviction on indictment, to a fine not exceeding $1,000,000 or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding five years, or to both.
@ref CBCA s 20(6), s 21.4(5) maximum punishment (src:270 src:417)
DECLARE `Maximum punishment for an offence in Parts I to VI` HAS
    `maximum fine in dollars` IS A NUMBER
    `maximum imprisonment in months` IS A MAYBE NUMBER

§§ `Where corporate records are kept -- Part IV`

-- s 20(1), s 20(5) and s 21.1(1) speak of the registered office and of "any other place in Canada designated
-- by the directors"; s 20(5.1) adds a place outside Canada. One enumeration serves all of them.
-- src:250 | 20 (1) A corporation shall prepare and maintain, at its registered office or at any other place in Canada designated by the directors, records containing
-- src:264 | (5) If accounting records of a corporation are kept outside Canada, accounting records adequate to enable the directors to ascertain the financial position of the corporation with reasonable accuracy on a quarterly basis shall be kept at the registered office or any other place in Canada designated by the directors.
-- src:266 | (5.1) Despite subsections (1) and (5), but subject to the Income Tax Act, the Excise Tax Act, the Customs Act and any other Act administered by the Minister of National Revenue, a corporation may keep all or any of its corporate records and accounting records referred to in subsection (1) or (2) at a place outside Canada, if
@ref CBCA s 20(1), s 20(5), s 20(5.1) place where records are kept (src:250 src:264 src:266)
DECLARE `Place where corporate records are kept (Part IV)` IS ONE OF
    `at the registered office of the corporation`
    `at another place in Canada designated by the directors`
    `at a place in Canada that the directors have not designated`
    `at a place outside Canada`

§§ `The facts every solvency test of Part V turns on`

-- Sections 34(2), 35(3), 36(2), 38(3) and 42 each forbid a step (buying shares, redeeming them, reducing
-- stated capital, paying a dividend) "if there are reasonable grounds for believing" that the corporation
-- is, or would after the step be, unable to pay its liabilities as they become due, or that the realizable
-- value of its assets would after the step be less than a sum that differs from section to section. The
-- facts are the same; each section reads only the fields it names, and says so.
-- The Act asks whether there are reasonable grounds for believing a shortfall. The corporation's honest and
-- reasonable estimates of the figures are the grounds: where they show a shortfall, there are reasonable
-- grounds (fork cb1-F7 in NOTES.md). The first field is a plain yes or no, because "unable to pay its
-- liabilities as they become due" is a judgement the Act hands to the person assessing the corporation.
-- src:572 | (2) A corporation shall not make any payment to purchase or otherwise acquire shares issued by it if there are reasonable grounds for believing that
-- src:573 | (a) the corporation is, or would after the payment be, unable to pay its liabilities as they become due; or
-- src:574 | (b) the realizable value of the corporation’s assets would after the payment be less than the aggregate of its liabilities and stated capital of all classes.
-- src:585 | (3) A corporation shall not make any payment to purchase or acquire under subsection (1) shares issued by it if there are reasonable grounds for believing that
-- src:586 | (a) the corporation is, or would after the payment be, unable to pay its liabilities as they become due; or
-- src:587 | (b) the realizable value of the corporation’s assets would after the payment be less than the aggregate of
-- src:588 | (i) its liabilities, and
-- src:589 | (ii) the amount required for payment on a redemption or in a liquidation of all shares the holders of which have the right to be paid before the holders of the shares to be purchased or acquired, to the extent that the amount has not been included in its liabilities.
-- src:593 | (2) A corporation shall not make any payment to purchase or redeem any redeemable shares issued by it if there are reasonable grounds for believing that
-- src:594 | (a) the corporation is, or would after the payment be, unable to pay its liabilities as they become due; or
-- src:595 | (b) the realizable value of the corporation’s assets would after the payment be less than the aggregate of
-- src:596 | (i) its liabilities, and
-- src:597 | (ii) the amount that would be required to pay the holders of shares that have a right to be paid, on a redemption or in a liquidation, rateably with or before the holders of the shares to be purchased or redeemed, to the extent that the amount has not been included in its liabilities.
-- src:608 | (3) A corporation shall not reduce its stated capital for any purpose other than the purpose mentioned in paragraph (1)(c) if there are reasonable grounds for believing that
-- src:609 | (a) the corporation is, or would after the reduction be, unable to pay its liabilities as they become due; or
-- src:610 | (b) the realizable value of the corporation’s assets would thereby be less than the aggregate of its liabilities.
-- src:654 | 42 A corporation shall not declare or pay a dividend if there are reasonable grounds for believing that
-- src:655 | (a) the corporation is, or would after the payment be, unable to pay its liabilities as they become due; or
-- src:656 | (b) the realizable value of the corporation’s assets would thereby be less than the aggregate of its liabilities and stated capital of all classes.
@ref CBCA s 34(2), s 35(3), s 36(2), s 38(3), s 42 solvency facts (src:572-574 src:585-589 src:593-597 src:608-610 src:654-656)
DECLARE `Facts for a solvency test in Part V` HAS
    `reasonable grounds exist for believing that the corporation is, or would after the step be, unable to pay its liabilities as they become due` IS A BOOLEAN
    `realizable value of the corporation's assets after the step, in dollars` IS A NUMBER
    `liabilities of the corporation, in dollars` IS A NUMBER
    `stated capital of all classes, in dollars` IS A NUMBER
    `amount required, on a redemption or in a liquidation, to pay the holders of shares that must be paid before the shares to be acquired and not included in the liabilities, in dollars` IS A NUMBER
    `amount required, on a redemption or in a liquidation, to pay the holders of shares that must be paid rateably with or before the shares to be acquired and not included in the liabilities, in dollars` IS A NUMBER
    `the corporation is an open-end mutual fund` IS A BOOLEAN
